# PART 3 — NON-NEGOTIABLE ENGINEERING STANDARDS

## 3.1 Complete vertical implementation

Every feature must include all applicable layers:

1. User interface and interactions.
2. Domain logic and business rules.
3. Input validation and data transformation.
4. State management and undo/redo where appropriate.
5. Persistence or actual backend integration.
6. Authentication and authorization where required.
7. Loading, success, cancellation, timeout, and failure handling.
8. Automated tests and integration tests.
9. Browser-level verification.
10. Documentation and configuration.

A visible button does not prove that a feature works. A frontend connected only to temporary memory does not prove persistence. A mock service does not prove a real integration.

If a layer is unnecessary, document why rather than adding artificial complexity.

## 3.2 Code quality

- Use strict TypeScript and explicit domain types.
- Avoid `any`, unsafe casts, unnecessary duplication, and loosely defined data structures.
- Separate business logic from UI components.
- Centralize graph rules, schema definitions, and validation.
- Use cohesive modules, clear naming, and consistent error handling.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies and unjustified rewrites.
- Follow existing project conventions.
- Remove unused code and resolve relevant warnings.
- Document important architectural and security decisions.

## 3.3 No fake functionality

Do not leave:

- Placeholder buttons presented as complete features.
- Disconnected controls.
- Hardcoded responses presented as real integrations.
- Mock services silently enabled in production.
- In-memory-only behavior presented as persistent storage.
- Fake successful operations.
- Silent exception handling.
- Unfinished critical TODOs.
- Undocumented manual workarounds.
- Features that require editing source code to perform ordinary configuration.

When required infrastructure is unavailable, implement what can be completed safely and document the exact blocker. Never manufacture a successful result.

## 3.4 Safe changes

- Never silently corrupt, discard, or overwrite user data.
- Confirm destructive operations and allow cancellation.
- Preserve existing data if an import or replacement fails.
- Make multi-step operations atomic where appropriate.
- Integrate with undo/redo when logically reversible.
- Prevent stale updates, race conditions, and duplicate side effects.
- Preserve existing functionality.
- Do not commit, push, deploy, delete production data, or modify live infrastructure without authorization.

